# Appendix A — The Boot Splash & the Four Doors

Chapter 1 walked you through booting the console. This appendix is the map you
keep open beside it: what the splash shows, what each door is *for*, and the one
fact that ties them together. Read Chapter 1 to learn it; read this to look it up.

## The splash

When Motoi starts it paints its name, a one-line subtitle, and four labelled
doors laid left to right:

```
                        M O T O I
                     a small console

          ┌────────┐  ┌────────┐  ┌────────┐  ┌────────┐
          │  IDE   │  │ MUSIC  │  │ ANIM.  │  │  GAME  │
          └────────┘  └────────┘  └────────┘  └────────┘
             talk       compose      tween        play
```

The splash is the console saying *I am on, and here is where you can begin*. It
boots in under a second. There is nothing to configure and nothing to install —
the machine is whole the moment the doors appear.

## The one fact that ties the doors together

**The four doors are not four programs.** They are four front porches on the same
house. There is exactly one machine underneath — one screen, one synth, one tick,
one sprite table, one memory. A door is just a workspace tuned for one kind of
making, with the right parts already wired up in front of you.

- The synth you play behind **Music** is the same synth a game beeps through
  behind **Game**.
- The sprite you tween behind **Animation** is the same sprite you push with a
  button behind **Game**.
- A song you compose behind **Music** can play inside a game you build behind
  **Game**, because it is the same sound chip.

Move between doors any time. Nothing you make behind one is locked away from the
others. This is why the book teaches the *machine*, not four separate apps.

## The four doors, as a reference

| Door | One word | What is wired up in front of you | Book chapters |
|---|---|---|---|
| **IDE** | talk | The REPL — a prompt that reads an expression and prints its value. | 1, 2 |
| **Music** | compose | The synth, the beat grid, and the transport. | 8, 9, 14 |
| **Animation** | tween | The tween engine and the easing curves. | 10 |
| **Game** | play | The tick loop, input, and the sprite table. | 5, 6, 7, 11, 12, 13 |

### IDE — the prompt

The most honest door. You type an expression; the console reads it, evaluates it,
and prints what came back, then loops for the next one — Read, Eval, Print, Loop.
Everything else in the console can be driven from here, because everything is a
verb you can call at the prompt. When in doubt, open the IDE and *ask the machine*.

### Music — the composer for sound

The synth, tempo, and transport already in front of you. Notes are numbers,
chords are lists of numbers, drum patterns are rows of ones and zeros, and a song
is a list you hand to the player. Chapters 8, 9, and 14 teach it; Appendix E is
the internals.

### Animation — the composer for motion-with-taste

The tween engine and the easing curves in front of you. Where the Game door moves
things by rules and physics, the Animation door moves them along *curves* — ease
in, ease out, a bounce at the end. Chapter 10 teaches it.

### Game — the composer for play

The tick loop, input, and the sprite table in front of you. This is where the
console turns the crank for you: `on-tick` runs your update sixty times a second,
`input/down?` reads the player's hand, and the sprite table holds every thing on
screen. Chapters 5, 6, 7, 11, 12, and 13 build up to a whole game here.

## What runs where — and what does not, at the bare prompt

A thing worth keeping straight while you read the book: some of the console's
powers are *host-driven*, meaning the running console pumps them for you. At the
bare IDE prompt, with no game loop turning, those do not fire on their own:

- **`on-tick`** — your update runs sixty times a second only when the Game door's
  loop is turning. At the bare prompt you call your update yourself (Chapter 13).
- **Input** — `input/down?` reads a real player's hand only in a live console.
  At the prompt there is no hand to read (Chapter 6).
- **The beat** — `at-beat` and the transport fire on the running clock, not at a
  bare prompt (Chapter 9).
- **Sound** — the synth *schedules* at the prompt and hands you the score to
  read; a live console drives the speaker (Chapters 8, 14).

None of this changes the logic you write. The console simply starts turning the
crank once you are behind the Game or Music door with a loop running.

